<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kumulipo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first recorded in the 18th century; printed by King Kalākaua in <a href="/years/1889">1889</a>; translated by Liliʻuokalani, published <a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kumulipo is the creation chant of Hawaiian religion, first recorded in the 18th century. It is also a genealogy of Hawaiian royalty. It was made in honour of the chief Kalaninuiamamao and passed down orally to his daughter Alapaʻiwahine. According to one account, his birth brought peace between the ʻI and Keawe families, cousins who had been feuding.</p></div>
